1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
 So, welcome back, we're going to continue and start talking about building our own functions.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,840
 So, again, we use the def keyword to define a function and then later we're going to invoke this.

3
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:17,920
 And there's a bit to it, we are defining the name of the function, in effect we're extending Python

4
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:22,120
 and creating new predefined things that we can use, except it's our code.

5
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:26,120
 It starts with a def keyword, has some optional arguments, which we'll see in a bit,

6
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:32,160
 that's what the parenthesis is, and then the name, and the function names follow the same rules as variable names.

7
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:36,840
 And then you have an indented block, whatever code you want to do, and then you have a deindented block,

8
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:39,800
 and that sort of defines the essence.

9
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:45,280
 The key thing here is this is not calling.

10
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:51,120
 It's not invoking, it's not executing, it's remembering, it's storing, it's figuring things out.

11
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:55,680
 So here is the output of a program that defines a function but then doesn't use it.

12
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:58,240
 So this is a sort of broken function.

13
00:00:58,240 --> 00:01:00,440
 So here we go, we start x equals five print.

14
00:01:00,440 --> 00:01:03,160
 You don't have to def, you have all the defs at the beginning.

15
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:04,600
 The def runs whenever.

16
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:06,960
 So, you know, out comes hello.

17
00:01:06,960 --> 00:01:11,640
 And then we define a function and this says, oh, oh, you want to make a new thing here.

18
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:12,520
 So I'll make a new thing.

19
00:01:12,520 --> 00:01:14,360
 It's kind of like a variable in a sense.

20
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:19,400
 And then it copies this stuff, copies it up there and says, later you probably are going to want to use this,

21
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:22,720
 so I'm going to remember it, so it doesn't do anything there.

22
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:27,840
 No output comes out, then it says print yo, and out comes yo.

23
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:32,080
 And then it adds two to x, so x is now seven, and then it prints x and there's no seven.

24
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:33,280
 There's seven.

25
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:36,320
 These print statements never ran.

26
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:37,200
 They never ran.

27
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:39,800
 Why? Because we did not invoke them down here.

28
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:42,960
 We defined them but didn't invoke them.

29
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:46,440
 So let's take a look at how you invoke a function, right?

30
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:48,200
 You define it and then you use it.

31
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:52,320
 Sometimes you define it once and use it once, but more commonly you define it once

32
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:53,880
 and use more than one time.

33
00:01:53,880 --> 00:01:55,440
 Again, the store and reuse pattern.

34
00:01:55,440 --> 00:02:00,200
 The def is the store and the invoking is the reuse.

35
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:07,080
 So here's just a slightly different version of that last program, and so now it's going to actually invoke it.

36
00:02:07,080 --> 00:02:11,480
 So x equals five, print hello, def, so out comes hello.

37
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:17,360
 This produces, the def produces no output, right, but because there's a deindent here,

38
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:22,280
 that is the entire blob of the code that is part of print lyrics.

39
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:25,640
 So it prints out yo, and now we're going to invoke.

40
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:27,200
 This is the call.

41
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:28,640
 We're going to call the function.

42
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:33,320
 Now the function goes up, let's clear this.

43
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:35,400
 So we're down to here.

44
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:38,640
 Now this like suspends at this place.

45
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:41,560
 It's like remember to come back to here when we're done.

46
00:02:41,560 --> 00:02:46,680
 Go up, run this code, and then come back, and then continue on.

47
00:02:46,680 --> 00:02:51,600
 So it like leaves like a breadcrumb of where it's supposed to come back to, and then it runs.

48
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:56,560
 And then the print lyrics, of course, produces the two lines of output.

49
00:02:56,560 --> 00:03:01,280
 And yeah, that should probably not have, they should be up there.

50
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:05,160
 And then x equals x plus two, which makes it seven, and then prints out seven.

51
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:11,200
 Okay, so this is the invoking, invoke or call the function.

52
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:15,960
 Okay, you defined it, and then later you called it.

53
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:22,320
 Now, in addition to just call and return and invoking, we can pass parameters in.

54
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:26,280
 And the example of the parameter is, in the max function we have to say,

55
00:03:26,280 --> 00:03:30,080
 this is the thing I want you to find the maximum of, the largest thing.

56
00:03:30,080 --> 00:03:35,080
 And part of it is, in the whole store and reuse pattern, we have a few lines of code.

57
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:39,920
 But sometimes we want to do ever so slightly different things in the different invocations.

58
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:47,880
 And so we use the arguments to subtly adjust, like finding the maximum is a general thing.

59
00:03:47,880 --> 00:03:49,840
 But what thing to find the maximum of,

60
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:55,960
 that makes a function that's much more useful and reusable in a lot more situations.

61
00:03:55,960 --> 00:03:58,440
 So arguments are the thing we passed in.

62
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:04,560
 And we define for our functions that we're going to build, we, on the def statement.

63
00:04:04,560 --> 00:04:08,760
 So we say def, greet, name a function, and then this is the arguments,

64
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:11,480
 the things that are coming in.

65
00:04:11,480 --> 00:04:17,000
 Now, this lang variable, in a sense, only exists during the life of the function.

66
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:18,880
 And it represents sort of a placeholder.

67
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:21,880
 It's not a real variable in the same sense.

68
00:04:21,880 --> 00:04:28,320
 It's a placeholder that refers to how you touch that first parameter that's sitting in there.

69
00:04:28,320 --> 00:04:33,960
 Okay, and so lang, so lang is our first parameter, whatever it is.

70
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:37,160
 We don't need to see this part down here right now.

71
00:04:37,160 --> 00:04:41,520
 All we know is we're going to make a function, and we're going to take a parameter.

72
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:46,880
 And this lang is the placeholder that tells us what that parameter is, okay?

73
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:50,880
 So within the function, we're going to check to see if the language is Spanish.

74
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:55,520
 If we are print hello, else if the language is French, print bonjour.

75
00:04:55,520 --> 00:04:56,680
 Otherwise, print hello.

76
00:04:56,680 --> 00:05:02,280
 We have a very highly simplified language translation system here.

77
00:05:02,280 --> 00:05:08,360
 So the def, of course, does nothing, except remembers that and defines the concept greet.

78
00:05:08,360 --> 00:05:14,640
 So that comes down, and now we're going to call it.

79
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:17,360
 And it says go look up the thing that I defined called greet.

80
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:19,720
 If you don't put this in, greet is going to give you a traceback.

81
00:05:19,720 --> 00:05:22,360
 But because you extended and named it greet.

82
00:05:22,360 --> 00:05:27,280
 So it runs in, it starts, suspends the code here, starts up here.

83
00:05:27,280 --> 00:05:32,600
 But then lang is now an alias to en.

84
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:39,560
 So now we can run if that is es, else if, I'm getting it all wrong now.

85
00:05:39,560 --> 00:05:46,160
 All right, so en comes in as lang, we're coming in the code.

86
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:51,700
 If it's not es, it's not fr, else, it prints hello.

87
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:53,440
 And then it comes back to the next line.

88
00:05:54,960 --> 00:05:58,420
 And then we call it again, and this time es is lang, and so

89
00:05:58,420 --> 00:06:02,240
 it runs this code and prints hola.

90
00:06:02,240 --> 00:06:07,920
 And then next time it calls with this, and then prints bonjour.

91
00:06:07,920 --> 00:06:09,040
 You get the idea.

92
00:06:09,040 --> 00:06:14,320
 So this is a placeholder to, so that on the successive calls or

93
00:06:14,320 --> 00:06:18,000
 invokes, invocating, invocation of the function,

94
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:22,240
 we can get at whatever the programmer put in as that first parameter.

95
00:06:22,240 --> 00:06:24,960
 And so we are saying in this definition,

96
00:06:24,960 --> 00:06:29,880
 we are ready to receive a first parameter, please call us with a parameter.

97
00:06:29,880 --> 00:06:32,480
 And then we will be able to do something slightly different for

98
00:06:32,480 --> 00:06:33,200
 the different values.

99
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:36,920
 So this is a reusable bit of function that prints hello in

100
00:06:36,920 --> 00:06:39,760
 three different languages, and then we tell it what language

101
00:06:39,760 --> 00:06:42,160
 at the moment that we're actually invoking it.

102
00:06:42,160 --> 00:06:46,920
 So that's putting stuff into the function.

103
00:06:46,920 --> 00:06:51,400
 Now getting stuff back out is the concept of returning.

104
00:06:51,400 --> 00:06:54,880
 In the return statement, the return statement

105
00:06:54,880 --> 00:07:00,520
 is an executable statement that does two basic things.

106
00:07:00,520 --> 00:07:04,120
 The first thing that it does is it finishes.

107
00:07:04,120 --> 00:07:07,160
 Now this is a one line function, so that's kind of redundant.

108
00:07:07,160 --> 00:07:11,360
 But when Python goes into the return statement,

109
00:07:11,360 --> 00:07:13,880
 it doesn't continue on to the next line.

110
00:07:13,880 --> 00:07:15,000
 It just returns.

111
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:18,680
 That is the end of the invocation of that particular function.

112
00:07:18,680 --> 00:07:21,320
 But even more importantly, it takes as its parameter.

113
00:07:21,320 --> 00:07:24,040
 You can say return without a parameter, and

114
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:28,760
 it will stop the execution of the function, kind of like a break does for a loop.

115
00:07:28,760 --> 00:07:30,000
 It's kind of a break for a loop.

116
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:33,160
 Get out, we're done, don't run that next line, get out.

117
00:07:33,160 --> 00:07:38,120
 But it also allows the specification of what you want as the residual value in

118
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:39,160
 an expression.

119
00:07:39,160 --> 00:07:42,160
 So we're doing a print, and then we're saying greet.

120
00:07:42,160 --> 00:07:48,200
 And what's going to show up here is whatever this function does in its return statement.

121
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:50,600
 And so that prints hello.

122
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:54,280
 We call it again, it prints hello again, okay?

123
00:07:54,280 --> 00:08:02,880
 And so basically, the return statement is, I call this the residual value.

124
00:08:02,880 --> 00:08:06,440
 It's like what shows up here when the function is all done?

125
00:08:06,440 --> 00:08:07,960
 And it's the string hello.

126
00:08:07,960 --> 00:08:13,680
 We call the functions that return values fruitful because they produce something.

127
00:08:13,680 --> 00:08:16,800
 But you don't have to, you can just say return.

128
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:18,480
 Or you don't even have to have a return statement.

129
00:08:18,480 --> 00:08:21,360
 It goes to the last line of the function and it does a return automatically at

130
00:08:21,360 --> 00:08:22,840
 the last line of the function.

131
00:08:22,840 --> 00:08:26,280
 So here's a little bit of a rewrite of our little language program.

132
00:08:26,280 --> 00:08:29,040
 We are going to create a greeting program.

133
00:08:29,040 --> 00:08:31,720
 We're going to take the language as the first parameter.

134
00:08:31,720 --> 00:08:34,560
 And instead of just doing a print statement, which is what we did before,

135
00:08:34,560 --> 00:08:38,760
 this is now more like a function cuz it takes some input and

136
00:08:38,760 --> 00:08:42,480
 produces some output as a return rather than just printing.

137
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:44,800
 It's a little tacky for a function to print.

138
00:08:44,800 --> 00:08:51,200
 And so here we return hola, bonjour, and hello based on the right thing.

139
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:56,120
 So now we say print greet en, so it runs the code once, lang is en.

140
00:08:56,120 --> 00:09:00,120
 And then it runs this code and the residual value is hello.

141
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:02,280
 So it says hello, Glenn.

142
00:09:02,280 --> 00:09:07,880
 And similarly, when it runs this code, it passes es in as lang.

143
00:09:07,880 --> 00:09:10,680
 It runs through and it runs this statement.

144
00:09:10,680 --> 00:09:12,720
 If there was more statements, it still wouldn't run them.

145
00:09:12,720 --> 00:09:21,120
 As soon as this return runs, that says that this bit right here is now hola.

146
00:09:21,120 --> 00:09:22,440
 So hola, Sally.

147
00:09:22,440 --> 00:09:25,080
 And the same with French, goes in, runs again.

148
00:09:25,080 --> 00:09:28,680
 Out comes the return statement, and then bonjour, Michael.

149
00:09:28,680 --> 00:09:32,720
 So you see how we can control as we're writing the application.

150
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:37,120
 We can control as we're writing the function what the residual value that we

151
00:09:37,120 --> 00:09:40,800
 want to see in whatever expression is calling us.

152
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:43,560
 Sometimes we have returns and sometimes we don't have returns.

153
00:09:43,560 --> 00:09:51,200
 So if you think of the max code that we talked about before,

154
00:09:51,200 --> 00:09:54,720
 we can kind of see that somewhere inside that max code, there's a return.

155
00:09:54,720 --> 00:09:57,440
 And that's how it communicates the w back to us.

156
00:09:57,440 --> 00:10:00,200
 So we pass in his argument, hello world.

157
00:10:00,200 --> 00:10:03,360
 It comes in as a parameter and it's going to loop through this imp somewhere.

158
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:06,760
 It's going to loop over and over into imp.

159
00:10:06,760 --> 00:10:08,920
 And then at some point, it's going to figure something out and

160
00:10:08,920 --> 00:10:12,560
 tell us what it wants to send back to us is a return statement.

161
00:10:12,560 --> 00:10:17,160
 And so the w comes back and gets assigned into big.

162
00:10:17,160 --> 00:10:22,280
 You can have more than one parameter, and they're just in order.

163
00:10:22,280 --> 00:10:24,560
 The first one and the second one, three and five.

164
00:10:24,560 --> 00:10:28,680
 So three becomes a and five becomes b, and away we go.

165
00:10:28,680 --> 00:10:32,560
 So we just use this to add two numbers, and so three plus five is eight.

166
00:10:32,560 --> 00:10:38,240
 So you get as many as you like, and the order matters.

167
00:10:38,240 --> 00:10:41,640
 And if you do things like you tell it you want parameters and

168
00:10:41,640 --> 00:10:45,640
 you don't give it to them, then that'll become a traceback and it'll blow up.

169
00:10:45,640 --> 00:10:48,920
 You can also talk about optional parameters later.

170
00:10:48,920 --> 00:10:51,120
 So you don't have to have return values, and

171
00:10:51,120 --> 00:10:55,040
 that means that you simply don't call return with a value.

172
00:10:55,040 --> 00:11:01,280
 And return is always implicitly happening as the last line of the function.

173
00:11:01,280 --> 00:11:08,040
 So that's kind of the basics of how functions operate.

174
00:11:08,040 --> 00:11:11,720
 But I don't want you to get too excited about writing functions.

175
00:11:11,720 --> 00:11:15,080
 Some programming classes are like, got to write a function,

176
00:11:15,080 --> 00:11:16,320
 got to write a function.

177
00:11:16,320 --> 00:11:20,280
 Functions, to be clear, are a very powerful mechanism.

178
00:11:20,280 --> 00:11:25,400
 And as we write programs, 150, 200,000, 200 lines of code,

179
00:11:25,400 --> 00:11:27,960
 1,000 lines of code, 10,000 lines of code,

180
00:11:27,960 --> 00:11:30,720
 the concept of a function is really important.

181
00:11:30,720 --> 00:11:32,800
 We would go crazy if we didn't have functions.

182
00:11:32,800 --> 00:11:36,920
 But if you're only writing 20 lines of code, forcing yourself to write

183
00:11:36,920 --> 00:11:39,240
 a function is kind of pointless.

184
00:11:39,240 --> 00:11:45,040
 So don't worry about maybe the lack of urge to use this.

185
00:11:45,040 --> 00:11:47,200
 We are calling lots of predefined functions, and

186
00:11:47,200 --> 00:11:49,640
 we will for the next couple of lectures.

187
00:11:49,640 --> 00:11:53,080
 There will be a time when you go like, I'm sick and tired of repeating myself.

188
00:11:53,080 --> 00:11:54,280
 Yeah, time to write a function.

189
00:11:54,280 --> 00:11:58,880
 So that's why we don't push functions prematurely.

190
00:11:58,880 --> 00:12:03,560
 We just want you to know what they are, use them, and at some moment you'll be

191
00:12:03,560 --> 00:12:04,880
 like, I want to define one.

192
00:12:04,880 --> 00:12:06,760
 But don't worry about, it might take a while.

193
00:12:06,760 --> 00:12:09,400
 Before you really want to define a function.

194
00:12:09,400 --> 00:12:13,440
 So, that kind of summarizes our lecture on functions.

195
00:12:13,440 --> 00:12:15,920
 And up next we're going to do iterations.

196
00:12:15,920 --> 00:12:25,920
 [MUSIC]

